GETC(3)                        P32W Unix                         GETC(3)

NAME
       getc, fgetc, getchar, ungetc, fgets - read characters or lines

SYNOPSIS
       #include <stdio.h>

       int getc(FILE *f);
       int fgetc(FILE *f);
       int getchar(void);
       int ungetc(int c, FILE *f);
       char *fgets(char *s, int n, FILE *f);

DESCRIPTION
       fgetc returns the next byte of the stream f, as an unsigned
       char (0 to 255) converted to int, or EOF at the end of the
       input or on an error. getc is the same function (not a
       macro); getchar is getc(stdin).

       When its buffer is empty, the stream reads up to BUFSIZ (512)
       bytes with one read(2); on a terminal a read returns a line
       at a time. Before stdin's buffer is refilled, stdout is
       flushed.

       ungetc pushes the character c back onto f, to be returned by
       the next fgetc (or fread). One character can be pushed back;
       a second ungetc replaces the first. It clears the end-of-file
       flag. EOF cannot be pushed back. fseek(3) forgets a pushed-
       back character.

       fgets reads characters into s until it has read a newline or
       n - 1 characters or reaches the end of the input; the newline
       is kept, and a NUL ends the string.

RETURN VALUE
       fgetc, getc and getchar return the character or EOF. Use
       feof(3) and ferror(3) to tell the end of the input from an
       error. ungetc returns c, or EOF if c was EOF.

       fgets returns s, or NULL if the input ended (or failed) before
       a single character was read.

EXAMPLES
       int c;
       while ((c = getchar()) != EOF)
           putchar(c);

       char line[256];
       while (fgets(line, sizeof line, f) != NULL)
           fputs(line, stdout);

SEE ALSO
       read(2), stdio(3), putc(3), fread(3), ferror(3)

BUGS
       There is no gets.

       The end-of-file flag does not stop later reads: each call
       after the end tries read(2) again, so on a terminal a getchar
       after ^D waits for more input.

       fgets with n of 1 (or less) returns NULL rather than an empty
       string. A line longer than n - 1 characters comes back in
       pieces, the last ending in the newline.
